  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nOver the next few days, a series of shortwaves are expected to move \nthrough the region bringing the chance for heavy rain and severe \nweather. The shortwave that brought the severe weather to Northern \nOhio and Northwestern Pennsylvania has moved off to the east and \nthere will be a brief period of dry weather until after midnight \ntonight. There is low probability of showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping across the southern tier of counties in the CWA, but \nconfidence is low due to the thunderstorms this morning across \nCentral Ohio working over the atmosphere. Another shortwave will \nenter the region late tonight and move through by tomorrow morning. \nCurrent expectation is that the showers and thunderstorms will be in \nthe western and southern portion of the CWA. There will be potential \nfor heavy rain to bring localized flash flooding to the areas that \nget precipitation, but the limiting factor is that that area has not \nreceived much precipitation over the past couple of days. Will need \nto monitor the development of the precipitation and adjust as \nneeded. Additionally, there will be a threat of damaging winds with \nthese storms as they moved through. Instability will be lacking \ngiven the storms will be moving through overnight, but there will be \naround 40 knots of bulk shear across the region that could support \nstrong to severe winds. As the shortwave moves out of the area to \nthe east, precipitation should end by mid morning and dry weather \nis expected through midday. \n\nAnother round of showers and thunderstorms are expected late \ntomorrow afternoon into the evening as another stronger shortwave \nmoves into the region from the northwest. Current model guidance \nsuggests an MCS will be moving through Northern Illinois through \nIndiana and into Central Ohio. There will be ample instability \nahead of this feature along with decent lapse rates and bulk shear \nthat suggests that it will continue into Ohio. The main question \nwith this would be where it will set up and if it reaches further \nnorth than a line from Bowling Green down through Canton, OH. \nRegardless, strong to damaging wind gusts will be plausible along \nthis feature from the afternoon into the early evening as it exits \nto the southeast. Heavy rain and flooding could be a threat as well \ngiven the previous days precipitation, though confidence is low in \nflooding occurring given the reasons stated for the previous night. \nSevere weather threat should end by late Tuesday evening and \nprimarily dry weather is expected overnight. \n\nWednesday evening into the early overnight hours, another shortwave \nand surface low will move through the region with another round of \nshowers and thunderstorms. There's still some uncertainty on how \nthis system will play out, but with the current run of models, it \ndoes not appear to be as impactful as far as severe weather goes. \nGenerally, instability will be lower across the region, but with \ndecent shear could support some stronger wind gusts. Precipitation \nwill end late Wednesday night as the surface low moves off to the \nsoutheast and high pressure builds in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will begin to build into the region from the northwest \nThursday afternoon. There is a low probability for showers across \nthe southern portion of the CWA into Central Ohio on Thursday \nevening and how far north these get will be dependent on how far \nsouth the high has built in at that time. Generally, the majority of \nthe area will be dry with everyone being dry through Saturday as the \nhigh builds overhead and moves eastward. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nUnsettled weather returns to the region late Saturday evening \nthrough the remainder of the weekend. The upper level ridge will \nbreakdown and northwesterly zonal flow is likely across the region \nand a few shortwaves are expected to move through the weekend. Given \nthe event is several days away details are still uncertain on if \nthere will be a severe or flooding threat.",
